{%- comment -%}
  Product — ported from ProductPage.jsx: gallery + lightbox, buy box with option
  swatches, stock line, quantity, add-to-cart, trust band, and the four tabs.

  Two honest departures from the React page, both called out in the README:

  1. REVIEWS are read-only here. The React page let a visitor "write a review"
     into localStorage — on a real storefront that renders a review nobody else
     can ever see, which reads as a working feature and isn't one. So this
     template DISPLAYS ratings from the standard reviews.rating /
     reviews.rating_count metafields (what review apps write to) and omits the
     submit form. Install a reviews app to collect them for real.

  2. VOLUME PRICE BREAKS are off by default. The theme cannot apply a discount —
     only Shopify Discounts can. Showing a "save 15%" table the checkout does not
     honour is a promise the store would break. Turn it on in theme settings
     ONLY once matching automatic discounts exist.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  assign current_variant = product.selected_or_first_available_variant
  assign rating = product.metafields.reviews.rating.value.rating
  assign rating_count = product.metafields.reviews.rating_count.value
  assign bestseller = false
  if settings.bestseller_tag != blank and product.tags contains settings.bestseller_tag
    assign bestseller = true
  endif
  assign on_sale = false
  if product.compare_at_price > product.price
    assign on_sale = true
  endif
-%}

<main id="main" class="pdp" data-mts-product>
  <script type="application/json" data-mts-product-json>{{ product.variants | json }}</script>

  <div class="wrap pdp__crumbs">
    {% render 'mts-breadcrumbs', parent_title: section.settings.parent_label, parent_url: section.settings.parent_url %}
  </div>

  <div class="wrap pdp__top">
    {%- comment -%} gallery {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="pdp-gallery" data-mts-gallery>
      <div class="pdp-gallery__row">
        {%- comment -%}
          Thumbnails are visible again.

          They were hidden on request ("the small boxes should not appear"),
          but that turned out to conflict with a second requirement: a product
          with 4-7 photos then had no way to reach any of them, so the gallery
          looked as though the images had never been migrated. Visible
          thumbnails are also how the original store presents these.

          They are still the mechanism the variant picker uses — paintImage()
          clicks the matching thumb so the main image, the active highlight and
          the lightbox index all move together.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- if product.media.size > 1 -%}
          <div class="pdp-gallery__thumbs">
            {%- comment -%}
              No limit. It was 6, but variants point at images anywhere in the
              set — a 9-image product had variant photos with no thumb to
              activate, so switching colour could not highlight the right one.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            {%- for media in product.media -%}
              {%- comment -%}
                data-media-id lets the variant picker point at an exact image.
                Matching on the URL would be fragile — Shopify rewrites
                filenames on collision and appends its own size/version query.
              {%- endcomment -%}
              <button class="pdp-gallery__thumb{% if forloop.first %} is-active{% endif %}"
                      data-mts-thumb data-full="{{ media | image_url: width: 1600 }}"
                      data-media-id="{{ media.id }}"
                      data-index="{{ forloop.index0 }}" aria-label="{{ 'products.product.view_image' | t }}">
                <img src="{{ media | image_url: width: 144 }}" alt="" width="72" height="72" loading="lazy">
              </button>
            {%- endfor -%}
          </div>
        {%- endif -%}

        <button class="pdp-gallery__main" data-mts-gallery-main aria-label="{{ 'products.product.zoom' | t }}">
          {%- if product.featured_media -%}
            {%- comment -%}
              This is the LCP element of every product page, and it had no
              srcset — a 390px phone downloaded the same 1200px file as a
              desktop, which is roughly six times the pixels it can show.

              Sizes: `.pdp__top` is a 1.15fr / 0.85fr grid inside a 1280px
              `.wrap` with 24px side padding and a 54px gap, so the gallery
              column settles at ~680px on desktop; below the grid's breakpoint
              it is full-bleed. The 44px inner padding shrinks the visible art,
              not the box, so the box width is what `sizes` must describe.

              Kept in step with the preload in layout/theme.liquid — if the
              widths here change, change them there too or the browser
              preloads one file and paints another.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            <img src="{{ product.featured_media | image_url: width: 800 }}"
                 srcset="{{ product.featured_media | image_url: width: 400 }} 400w,
                         {{ product.featured_media | image_url: width: 600 }} 600w,
                         {{ product.featured_media | image_url: width: 800 }} 800w,
                         {{ product.featured_media | image_url: width: 1000 }} 1000w,
                         {{ product.featured_media | image_url: width: 1360 }} 1360w"
                 sizes="(min-width: 960px) 680px, 100vw"
                 alt="{{ product.featured_media.alt | default: product.title | escape }}"
                 width="600" height="600" data-mts-gallery-img
                 fetchpriority="high" decoding="async">
          {%- else -%}
            {{ 'product-1' | placeholder_svg_tag }}
          {%- endif -%}
          <div class="pdp-gallery__badges">
            {%- if bestseller -%}
              <span class="tag tag--best">{% render 'mts-icon', name: 'star', size: 11 %} {{ 'products.product.bestseller' | t }}</span>
            {%- endif -%}
            {%- if on_sale -%}<span class="tag tag--sale">{{ 'products.product.sale' | t }}</span>{%- endif -%}
            <span class="tag tag--out" data-mts-oos-badge {% if current_variant.available %}hidden{% endif %}>
              {{ 'products.product.out_of_stock_badge' | t }}
            </span>
          </div>
          <span class="pdp-gallery__zoom">{% render 'mts-icon', name: 'search', size: 16 %}</span>
        </button>
      </div>

      {%- assign key_specs = product.metafields.custom.key_specs.value -%}
      {%- if key_specs -%}
        <ul class="pdp-gallery__specs">
          {%- for spec in key_specs limit: 4 -%}
            <li>{% render 'mts-icon', name: 'check', size: 15 %}<span>{{ spec }}</span></li>
          {%- endfor -%}
        </ul>
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>

    {%- comment -%} buy column {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="pdp-buy">
      {%- comment -%}
        `product.collections.first` is whatever order Shopify returns, and for
        many products that is an INDUSTRY — so the eyebrow above the H1 read
        "Building & Construction" and the spec table's Category row said the
        same. Neither is the product's category; both are who might buy it.

        Same rule as the card and the chips: take the first collection that is
        an actual product category. `first_coll` feeds the eyebrow, the spec
        row, the chip de-duplication and the related-products fallback, so
        fixing it here fixes all four.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        The industries are read from the `industries` MENU rather than a frozen
        list. The hardcoded version had 22 handles and the store has more — it
        was missing `airconditioning-refrigeration-tapes`, so any product whose
        collections happened to return that first showed "Air Conditioning &
        Refrigeration Tapes" as its CATEGORY. Butyl Tape was one of them.

        Liquid does not guarantee the order of `product.collections`, so a skip
        list that is merely mostly-complete fails intermittently and looks like
        a random bug. Reading the menu means adding an industry can never
        reintroduce this.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- capture cat_skip -%},{% for l in linklists.industries.links %}{{ l.url | split: '/' | last | split: '?' | first }},{% endfor %}all,frontpage,bestsellers,{%- endcapture -%}
      {%- if linklists.industries.links.size == 0 -%}
        {%- comment -%} menu missing — fall back so the row never shows an industry {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- assign cat_skip = ',aerospace-defense,airconditioning-refrigeration-tapes,building-construction,display-signage,electronics-electrical,flooring,framing-insulation,glass-glazing,hvac-plumbing,joinery-kitchen-furniture,manufacturing,marine,marking-safety,nameplates,pool-spa,printing,roofing-gutters,sheathing-moisture-management,solar-energy,telecommunication,transport-automotive-rv,warehouse-packaging-logistics,windows-doors-decking,all,frontpage,bestsellers,' -%}
      {%- endif -%}
      {%- assign first_coll = nil -%}
      {%- for coll in product.collections -%}
        {%- if first_coll == nil -%}
          {%- assign c_needle = coll.handle | prepend: ',' | append: ',' -%}
          {%- unless cat_skip contains c_needle -%}
            {%- assign first_coll = coll -%}
          {%- endunless -%}
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- endfor -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        The category eyebrow above the H1 is gone on request. It was internal
        linking dressed as a label, and because it drew from the same
        collection lookup it frequently announced an INDUSTRY over the product
        name — "Air Conditioning & Refrigeration Tapes" sitting above Butyl
        Tape. `first_coll` is still computed: the Specifications table and the
        related-products fallback both use it.
      {%- endcomment -%}

      <h1 class="pdp-buy__title">{{ product.title }}</h1>

      {%- comment -%}
        The rating comes from our own metafields, not Judge.me's preview badge.

        Judge.me is installed but holds zero reviews, so its badge rendered an
        empty strip on every product while 119 genuine reviews sat unused. Our
        stars are also server-rendered, which means they are in the HTML for
        Google to read — the app badge paints in later via JavaScript.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- if rating -%}
        <div class="pdp-buy__rating">
          <button class="pdp-buy__ratebtn" data-mts-goto-reviews aria-label="See reviews">
            {% render 'mts-stars', rating: rating, size: 16, show_count: false %}
          </button>
          <button class="pdp-buy__revlink" data-mts-goto-reviews>
            {%- if rating_count > 0 -%}
              {{ 'products.product.reviews_count' | t: count: rating_count }}
            {%- else -%}
              {{ 'products.product.no_reviews' | t }}
            {%- endif -%}
          </button>
        </div>
      {%- endif -%}

      <div class="pdp-buy__idline">
        {%- if product.vendor != blank -%}
          <span class="pdp-buy__idchip"><b>{{ 'products.product.brand' | t }}</b> {{ product.vendor }}</span>
        {%- endif -%}
        <span class="pdp-buy__idchip num" data-mts-sku-wrap {% if current_variant.sku == blank %}hidden{% endif %}>
          <b>{{ 'products.product.sku' | t }}</b> <span data-mts-sku>{{ current_variant.sku }}</span>
        </span>
      </div>

      <div class="pdp-buy__pricerow">
        {%- comment -%}
          When a quantity break applies, the headline price becomes the
          discounted one and the original is shown struck through beside it —
          otherwise the shopper sees the price silently drop with nothing to
          say why, and loses the saving as a reason to buy more.
          Hidden until a tier is active; mts-theme.js owns it.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <s class="pdp-buy__was num" data-mts-was hidden></s>
        <span class="pdp-buy__price num" data-mts-price>{{ current_variant.price | money }}</span>
        <span class="pdp-buy__gst">{{ 'products.product.inc_gst' | t }}</span>
      </div>
      <p class="pdp-buy__tax">
        {{ 'products.product.tax_line' | t }}
        {%- if product.price_varies %} &middot; Range {{ product.price_min | money }} – {{ product.price_max | money }}{% endif -%}
      </p>

      {%- form 'product', product, class: 'pdp-buybox', data-mts-product-form: true, novalidate: 'novalidate' -%}
        <input type="hidden" name="id" value="{{ current_variant.id }}" data-mts-variant-id>

        {%- unless product.has_only_default_variant -%}
          <div class="pdp-buy__section">
            {%- for option in product.options_with_values -%}
              {%- liquid
                assign oname = option.name | downcase
                assign is_colour = false
                if oname contains 'colour' or oname contains 'color'
                  assign is_colour = true
                endif
              -%}
              <div class="pdp-opt">
                <div class="pdp-opt__label">
                  <span>{{ option.name }}</span>
                  {%- if is_colour -%}
                    {%- comment -%} translate only the fallback — see mts-collection.liquid {%- endcomment -%}
                    <b data-mts-opt-selected="{{ forloop.index0 }}">
                      {%- if option.selected_value != blank -%}
                        {{ option.selected_value }}
                      {%- else -%}
                        {{ 'products.product.select_option' | t }}
                      {%- endif -%}
                    </b>
                  {%- endif -%}
                </div>

                {%- if is_colour -%}
                  <div class="pdp-opt__swatches">
                    {%- for value in option.values -%}
                      {%- capture swatch_fill -%}{% render 'mts-swatch-fill', value: value %}{%- endcapture -%}
                      {%- assign swatch_fill = swatch_fill | strip -%}
                      <button type="button"
                              class="pdp-swatch{% if option.selected_value == value %} is-active{% endif %}"
                              data-mts-opt="{{ forloop.parentloop.index0 }}" data-value="{{ value | escape }}"
                              title="{{ value | escape }}" aria-label="{{ value | escape }}"
                              aria-pressed="{% if option.selected_value == value %}true{% else %}false{% endif %}">
                        {%- if swatch_fill != blank -%}
                          <span class="pdp-swatch__dot" style="background: {{ swatch_fill }}"></span>
                        {%- else -%}
                          <span class="pdp-swatch__txt">{{ value }}</span>
                        {%- endif -%}
                      </button>
                    {%- endfor -%}
                  </div>
                {%- else -%}
                  <div class="pdp-opt__select">
                    <select data-mts-opt="{{ forloop.index0 }}" aria-label="{{ option.name | escape }}">
                      {%- for value in option.values -%}
                        <option value="{{ value | escape }}" {% if option.selected_value == value %}selected{% endif %}>{{ value }}</option>
                      {%- endfor -%}
                    </select>
                    {% render 'mts-icon', name: 'chevronDown', size: 16 %}
                  </div>
                {%- endif -%}
              </div>
            {%- endfor -%}
          </div>
        {%- endunless -%}

        {%- comment -%}
          Volume price breaks, read per product from the mts.volume_tiers
          metafield that scripts/push_tiers.py lifts off mytapestore.com.au.

          They are NOT one ladder for the whole catalogue: 81 of the 131
          products carry breaks and they run to nine different shapes — some
          start at a minimum of 6, one steps 10/15/20% — so the bands are
          per product and the rest of the catalogue shows nothing at all.

          `to: null` on the last band means "and up".
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- assign vt = product.metafields.mts.volume_tiers.value -%}
        {%- if settings.show_volume_tiers and vt.bands.size > 1 -%}
          <div class="pdp-buy__section pdp-vol" data-mts-volume>
            <div class="pdp-vol__head">
              {% render 'mts-icon', name: 'tag', size: 14 %} {{ 'products.product.volume_heading' | t }}
            </div>
            <div class="pdp-vol__row" role="group" aria-label="{{ 'products.product.volume_aria' | t }}">
              {%- for band in vt.bands -%}
                {%- liquid
                  assign off = band.off | round
                  assign keep = 100 | minus: off

                  # Add half a cent before the integer divide. Liquid truncates,
                  # so 5% off $36.86 would land on $35.01 where the original
                  # store — and every calculator — says $35.02.
                  assign tier_price = current_variant.price | times: keep | plus: 50 | divided_by: 100

                  if band.to == nil
                    assign lbl = 'products.product.pieces_plus' | t: from: band.from
                  elsif band.to == band.from
                    if band.from == 1
                      assign lbl = 'products.product.pieces_one' | t: n: band.from
                    else
                      assign lbl = 'products.product.pieces_many' | t: n: band.from
                    endif
                  else
                    assign lbl = 'products.product.pieces_range' | t: from: band.from, to: band.to
                  endif
                -%}
                <button type="button" class="pdp-vtier{% if forloop.first %} is-active{% endif %}"
                        data-mts-tier="{{ band.from }}" data-off="{{ off }}"
                        aria-label="{{ lbl }}{% if off > 0 %} — {{ off }}% off{% endif %}">
                  <span class="pdp-vtier__top">
                    <b class="num" data-mts-tier-price>{{ tier_price | money }}</b>
                    {%- if off > 0 -%}
                      <span class="pdp-vtier__off">{{ 'products.product.percent_off' | t: percent: off }}</span>
                    {%- endif -%}
                  </span>
                  <span class="pdp-vtier__lbl">{{ lbl }}</span>
                </button>
              {%- endfor -%}
            </div>
          </div>
        {%- endif -%}

        <div class="pdp-buy__section">
          <div class="pdp-buy__stock">
            <span class="pdp-buy__instock" data-mts-instock {% unless current_variant.available %}hidden{% endunless %}>
              <span class="pdp-buy__dot"></span> {{ 'products.product.in_stock' | t }}
            </span>
            <span class="pdp-buy__oos" data-mts-oos {% if current_variant.available %}hidden{% endif %}>
              {{ 'products.product.out_of_stock' | t }}
            </span>
          </div>

          {%- comment -%}
            MINIMUM ORDER QUANTITY.

            `min` on the volume_tiers metafield is the original store's
            per-product minimum, carried over from the WooCommerce plugin's
            `data-minimum`. It is not a pricing hint — it is the smallest
            quantity the shop will sell, and the first band always starts
            there (a 6-minimum masking tape's first row IS "6 pieces").

            112 products are min 1, 10 are min 2, 3 are min 4, 7 are min 6.

            Read it independently of `settings.show_volume_tiers`: hiding the
            price-break table is a merchandising choice, but the minimum is an
            order rule and has to hold either way.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- liquid
            assign min_qty = product.metafields.mts.volume_tiers.value.min | default: 1
            if min_qty < 1
              assign min_qty = 1
            endif
          -%}

          <div class="pdp-buy__actions">
            <div class="qty" role="group" aria-label="{{ 'products.product.quantity' | t }}">
              {%- comment -%}
                Starts disabled because the field opens at the floor. A button
                that silently does nothing is worse than one that says it is
                unavailable; JS re-enables it the moment the count rises.
              {%- endcomment -%}
              <button type="button" data-mts-qty-down disabled aria-disabled="true"
                      aria-label="{{ 'products.product.decrease' | t }}">
                {% render 'mts-icon', name: 'minus', size: 16 %}
              </button>
              <input class="num" type="text" inputmode="numeric" name="quantity" value="{{ min_qty }}"
                     data-mts-qty-min="{{ min_qty }}"
                     {% if min_qty > 1 %}aria-describedby="pdp-minqty-{{ section.id }}"{% endif %}
                     aria-label="{{ 'products.product.quantity' | t }}" data-mts-qty>
              <button type="button" data-mts-qty-up aria-label="{{ 'products.product.increase' | t }}">
                {% render 'mts-icon', name: 'plus', size: 16 %}
              </button>
            </div>

            <button type="submit" name="add" class="btn btn--brand btn--lg pdp-buy__add"
                    data-mts-add {% unless current_variant.available %}disabled{% endunless %}>
              <span data-mts-add-idle>{% render 'mts-icon', name: 'cart', size: 19 %} {{ 'products.product.add_to_cart' | t }}</span>
              <span data-mts-add-done hidden>{% render 'mts-icon', name: 'check', size: 19 %} {{ 'products.product.added_to_cart' | t }}</span>
            </button>

            {%- if settings.show_wishlist -%}
              <button type="button" class="pdp-buy__wish" data-mts-wish="{{ product.handle }}"
                      aria-label="{{ 'products.product.add_to_wishlist' | t }}" aria-pressed="false">
                {% render 'mts-icon', name: 'heart', size: 20 %}
              </button>
            {%- endif -%}
          </div>

          {%- comment -%}
            Say the rule, don't just enforce it. A minus button that refuses to
            go below 6 with no explanation reads as a broken control; naming the
            minimum turns a dead end into information.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- if min_qty > 1 -%}
            <p class="pdp-buy__minqty" id="pdp-minqty-{{ section.id }}">
              {% render 'mts-icon', name: 'info', size: 14 %}
              {{ 'products.product.min_qty' | t: n: min_qty }}
            </p>
          {%- endif -%}

          <p class="pdp-buy__linetotal" data-mts-linetotal-wrap>
            {{ 'products.product.total' | t }} <b class="num" data-mts-linetotal>{{ current_variant.price | money }}</b>
          </p>

          {%- comment -%}
            BUY IT NOW — our own button, not Shopify's dynamic checkout button.

            `{{ form | payment_button }}` was here, but its contents are chosen
            by Shopify: with Shop Pay enabled it renders "Buy with Shop" in
            Shop's purple, which is not wanted on this product page. The theme
            cannot relabel or restyle the inside of that button — Shopify owns
            it, because each one carries a signed wallet session.

            So this is a plain button again: add to cart, then go straight to
            /checkout. Same one-click intent, our wording, our colour, and it
            skips the cart page rather than stopping at it.

            The wallet buttons still exist on the CART page, where they are the
            faster-payment option rather than a competing brand on the PDP.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <button type="button" class="btn btn--dark btn--lg btn--block pdp-buy__buynow"
                  data-mts-buynow {% unless current_variant.available %}disabled{% endunless %}>
            {% render 'mts-icon', name: 'lock', size: 17 %} {{ 'products.product.buy_it_now' | t }}
          </button>

          <div class="pdp-buy__added" role="status" data-mts-added hidden>
            {% render 'mts-icon', name: 'check', size: 18 %}
            <span data-mts-added-text></span>
            <a href="{{ routes.cart_url }}">{{ 'products.product.view_cart' | t }}</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      {%- endform -%}

      <div class="pdp-trust">
        <ul class="pdp-trust__row">
          <li>{% render 'mts-icon', name: 'truck', size: 18 %}<span>{{ 'products.product.trust.dispatch' | t }}</span></li>
          <li>{% render 'mts-icon', name: 'refresh', size: 18 %}<span>{{ 'products.product.trust.returns' | t }}</span></li>
          <li>{% render 'mts-icon', name: 'lock', size: 18 %}<span>{{ 'products.product.trust.secure' | t }}</span></li>
          <li>{% render 'mts-icon', name: 'medal', size: 18 %}<span>{{ 'products.product.trust.price_guarantee' | t }}</span></li>
        </ul>
        <div class="pdp-trust__pay">
          <span class="pdp-trust__pay-label">{{ 'products.product.trust.we_accept' | t }}</span>
          <div class="pdp-buy__pay-marks">
            {%- assign marks = 'visa,mastercard,amex,paypal,shop-pay' | split: ',' -%}
            {%- for m in marks -%}
              <img src="{{ 'mts-pay-' | append: m | append: '.svg' | asset_url }}" alt="{{ m }}" width="32" height="20" loading="lazy">
            {%- endfor -%}
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="pdp-buy__meta">
          <a href="{{ section.settings.shipping_url }}">
            {{ 'products.product.trust.shipping_info' | t }} {% render 'mts-icon', name: 'chevronRight', size: 14 %}
          </a>
          <a href="{{ section.settings.returns_url }}">
            {{ 'products.product.trust.returns_info' | t }} {% render 'mts-icon', name: 'chevronRight', size: 14 %}
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  {%- comment -%} tabs {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="wrap pdp__tabs" data-mts-tabs>
    {%- comment -%}
      Each tab label is wrapped in an <h2>. The four panels are the page's
      top-level sections, so they belong in the outline at that level — and
      without them the document jumped straight from the H1 to the H3s inside
      the description, which reads as a broken hierarchy to a crawler.

      The <button role="tab"> stays inside the heading rather than being
      replaced by it: the tablist interaction, focus handling and aria-selected
      state all still work exactly as before. A heading wrapping a control is a
      standard accessible pattern.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="pdp-tabnav" role="tablist">
      <h2 class="pdp-tabnav__h"><button role="tab" aria-selected="true" class="pdp-tabnav__btn is-active" data-mts-tab="description">{{ 'products.product.tabs.description' | t }}</button></h2>
      <h2 class="pdp-tabnav__h"><button role="tab" aria-selected="false" class="pdp-tabnav__btn" data-mts-tab="specs">{{ 'products.product.tabs.specs' | t }}</button></h2>
      <h2 class="pdp-tabnav__h"><button role="tab" aria-selected="false" class="pdp-tabnav__btn" data-mts-tab="reviews">{{ 'products.product.tabs.reviews' | t }}{% if rating_count > 0 %} ({{ rating_count }}){% endif %}</button></h2>
      <h2 class="pdp-tabnav__h"><button role="tab" aria-selected="false" class="pdp-tabnav__btn" data-mts-tab="shipping">{{ 'products.product.tabs.shipping' | t }}</button></h2>
    </div>

    <div class="pdp-tabpanel" role="tabpanel">
      <div class="pdp-desc" data-mts-panel="description">
        {{ product.description }}
      </div>

      <div data-mts-panel="specs" hidden>
        <div class="pdp-specgrid">
          <table class="spec-table">
            <tbody>
              {%- if first_coll and first_coll.handle != 'all' -%}
                <tr><th>{{ 'products.product.specs.category' | t }}</th><td>{{ first_coll.title }}</td></tr>
              {%- endif -%}
              {%- comment -%}
                SKU removed from the spec table on request. It is an internal
                stock code — it tells a shopper nothing and sat second, above
                Size and Colour, which are the things they actually choose on.
                Still shown in the buy column next to the brand chip for anyone
                quoting a code over the phone.
              {%- endcomment -%}
              {%- unless product.has_only_default_variant -%}
                {%- for option in product.options_with_values -%}
                  <tr><th>{{ option.name }}</th><td>{{ option.values | uniq | join: ', ' }}</td></tr>
                {%- endfor -%}
              {%- endunless -%}
              <tr>
                <th>{{ 'products.product.specs.price' | t }}</th>
                <td class="num">
                  {%- if product.price_varies -%}{{ product.price_min | money }} – {{ product.price_max | money }}
                  {%- else -%}{{ product.price | money }}{%- endif -%}
                  {{ ' ' }}{{ 'products.product.inc_gst' | t }}
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th>{{ 'products.product.specs.availability' | t }}</th>
                <td>{% if product.available %}{{ 'products.product.specs.in_stock' | t }}{% else %}{{ 'products.product.specs.out_of_stock' | t }}{% endif %}</td>
              </tr>
              {%- if product.vendor != blank -%}
                <tr><th>{{ 'products.product.brand' | t }}</th><td>{{ product.vendor }}</td></tr>
              {%- endif -%}

              {%- comment -%}
                The real engineering data — thickness, temperature range,
                adhesive type, tensile strength. It used to sit in a
                "Specifications" block inside the DESCRIPTION, so the word
                appeared twice on the page and the tab showed only what Shopify
                itself knows. Lifted into `custom.specs` by
                scripts/lift_product_specs.py and rendered here, which is where
                a buyer looks for it.

                Rows already covered above (colour, size) are skipped so the
                table never states the same thing twice.
              {%- endcomment -%}
              {%- assign extra_specs = product.metafields.custom.specs.value -%}
              {%- if extra_specs -%}
                {%- assign shown = '|' -%}
                {%- unless product.has_only_default_variant -%}
                  {%- for option in product.options_with_values -%}
                    {%- assign shown = shown | append: option.name | downcase | append: '|' -%}
                  {%- endfor -%}
                {%- endunless -%}
                {%- for spec in extra_specs -%}
                  {%- assign key = spec.label | downcase | strip -%}
                  {%- assign singular = key | remove: 's' -%}
                  {%- unless shown contains key or shown contains singular -%}
                    <tr><th>{{ spec.label }}</th><td>{{ spec.value }}</td></tr>
                  {%- endunless -%}
                {%- endfor -%}
              {%- endif -%}
            </tbody>
          </table>

          {%- comment -%}
            "Recommended for" listed EVERY collection the product sits in, so a
            hook & loop roll recommended itself for "Display Signage" and, worse,
            for "Home page" — which is not a recommendation, it is a template.

            Industry collections and the structural ones are filtered out; what
            is left is genuine product categories. The whole block is skipped
            when nothing survives, rather than printing an empty heading.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- assign skip_handles = ',aerospace-defense,building-construction,display-signage,electronics-electrical,flooring,framing-insulation,glass-glazing,hvac-plumbing,joinery-kitchen-furniture,manufacturing,marine,marking-safety,nameplates,pool-spa,printing,roofing-gutters,sheathing-moisture-management,solar-energy,telecommunication,transport-automotive-rv,warehouse-packaging-logistics,windows-doors-decking,all,frontpage,bestsellers,' -%}
          {%- capture chips -%}
            {%- for coll in product.collections -%}
              {%- assign needle = coll.handle | prepend: ',' | append: ',' -%}
              {%- unless skip_handles contains needle or coll.handle == first_coll.handle -%}
                <a href="{{ coll.url }}">{{ coll.title }}</a>
              {%- endunless -%}
            {%- endfor -%}
          {%- endcapture -%}
          {%- assign chips = chips | strip -%}
          {%- if chips != blank -%}
            <div class="pdp-specgrid__ind">
              <h3>{{ 'products.product.specs.recommended_for' | t }}</h3>
              <div class="pdp-specbox__chips">{{ chips }}</div>
            </div>
          {%- endif -%}
        </div>
      </div>

      <div data-mts-panel="reviews" hidden>
        {%- comment -%}
          REVIEWS — rendered from our own data, not an app widget.

          The 119 genuine reviews from the WordPress store live on each product
          in `reviews.items` (a JSON list of {n,r,b,d}), with the aggregate in
          the standard `reviews.rating` / `reviews.rating_count` metafields that
          every review app and this theme already read.

          Judge.me is installed but empty, so its widget rendered a blank box
          here. When its import is done it can take this panel back over; until
          then real reviews beat an empty third-party frame.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- assign rev_items = product.metafields.reviews.items.value -%}
        <div class="pdp-rev">
          {%- if rev_items and rev_items.size > 0 -%}
            {%- liquid
              assign s5 = 0
              assign s4 = 0
              assign s3 = 0
              assign s2 = 0
              assign s1 = 0
              for rv in rev_items
                case rv.r
                  when 5
                    assign s5 = s5 | plus: 1
                  when 4
                    assign s4 = s4 | plus: 1
                  when 3
                    assign s3 = s3 | plus: 1
                  when 2
                    assign s2 = s2 | plus: 1
                  else
                    assign s1 = s1 | plus: 1
                endcase
              endfor
              assign rev_total = rev_items.size
            -%}

            <div class="pdp-rev__head">
              <div class="pdp-rev__score">
                <b class="num">{{ rating | round: 1 }}</b>
                {% render 'mts-stars', rating: rating, size: 17, show_count: false %}
                <span class="pdp-rev__count num">{{ 'products.product.reviews_count' | t: count: rev_total }}</span>
              </div>

              {%- comment -%}
                The distribution bars are not decoration: on a catalogue that is
                almost entirely 5-star, showing the spread is what makes the
                score look measured rather than curated.
              {%- endcomment -%}
              <ul class="pdp-rev__bars">
                {%- for pair in (1..5) reversed -%}
                  {%- liquid
                    case pair
                      when 5
                        assign n = s5
                      when 4
                        assign n = s4
                      when 3
                        assign n = s3
                      when 2
                        assign n = s2
                      else
                        assign n = s1
                    endcase
                    assign pct = 0
                    if rev_total > 0
                      assign pct = n | times: 100 | divided_by: rev_total
                    endif
                  -%}
                  <li class="pdp-rev__bar">
                    <span class="pdp-rev__bar-k num">{{ pair }}</span>
                    {% render 'mts-icon', name: 'star', size: 12 %}
                    <span class="pdp-rev__bar-track"><span class="pdp-rev__bar-fill" style="width:{{ pct }}%"></span></span>
                    <span class="pdp-rev__bar-n num">{{ n }}</span>
                  </li>
                {%- endfor -%}
              </ul>

              <button type="button" class="btn btn--brand pdp-rev__write" data-mts-rev-open>
                {% render 'mts-icon', name: 'star', size: 16 %} Write a review
              </button>
            </div>

            <ul class="pdp-rev__list" data-mts-rev-list>
              {%- for rv in rev_items -%}
                <li class="pdp-rev__item{% if forloop.index > 4 %} is-hidden{% endif %}" {% if forloop.index > 4 %}data-mts-rev-extra{% endif %}>
                  <div class="pdp-rev__by">
                    <span class="pdp-rev__avatar" aria-hidden="true">{{ rv.n | slice: 0 | upcase }}</span>
                    <div class="pdp-rev__meta">
                      <span class="pdp-rev__name">{{ rv.n }}</span>
                      <time class="pdp-rev__date num" datetime="{{ rv.d }}">{{ rv.d | date: "%-d %b %Y" }}</time>
                    </div>
                    {% render 'mts-stars', rating: rv.r, size: 14, show_count: false %}
                  </div>
                  <p class="pdp-rev__body">{{ rv.b | newline_to_br }}</p>
                </li>
              {%- endfor -%}
            </ul>

            {%- if rev_total > 4 -%}
              {%- assign more = rev_total | minus: 4 -%}
              <button type="button" class="btn btn--ghost pdp-rev__more" data-mts-rev-more
                      data-label-more="Show {{ more }} more review{% if more != 1 %}s{% endif %}"
                      data-label-less="Show fewer reviews">
                Show {{ more }} more review{% if more != 1 %}s{% endif %}
              </button>
            {%- endif -%}
          {%- else -%}
            <div class="pdp-rev__empty">
              {% render 'mts-icon', name: 'star', size: 30 %}
              <p>{{ 'products.product.no_reviews' | t }} — be the first to review this product.</p>
              <button type="button" class="btn btn--brand pdp-rev__write" data-mts-rev-open>Write a review</button>
            </div>
          {%- endif -%}

          {%- comment -%}
            WRITE A REVIEW
            Posts through Shopify's own contact form, which emails the store.
            That is deliberate: with no review API connected yet, a form that
            appeared to publish instantly would either lie or write to the
            visitor's own browser. This one genuinely reaches someone, and the
            review is added on moderation.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <div class="pdp-rev__form" data-mts-rev-form hidden>
            {%- form 'contact', class: 'pdp-rev__fields' -%}
              {%- if form.posted_successfully? -%}
                <p class="pdp-rev__sent" role="status">
                  {% render 'mts-icon', name: 'check', size: 20 %}
                  Thanks — your review has been sent to us and will appear once checked.
                </p>
              {%- else -%}
                {{ form.errors | default_errors }}
                <h3 class="pdp-rev__form-h">Review {{ product.title }}</h3>
                <input type="hidden" name="contact[subject]" value="Product review — {{ product.title | escape }}">
                <input type="hidden" name="contact[product_url]" value="{{ shop.url }}{{ product.url }}">

                <fieldset class="pdp-rev__rate">
                  <legend>Your rating</legend>
                  {%- comment -%}
                    Radios reversed in the DOM so a pure-CSS sibling selector can
                    light the stars up to the chosen one; the visual order is
                    restored with row-reverse. Works with no JavaScript at all.
                  {%- endcomment -%}
                  <div class="pdp-rev__stars-in">
                    {%- for i in (1..5) reversed -%}
                      <input type="radio" id="mts-star-{{ section.id }}-{{ i }}" name="contact[rating]" value="{{ i }}" required
                             {% if i == 5 %}checked{% endif %}>
                      <label for="mts-star-{{ section.id }}-{{ i }}" title="{{ i }} star{% if i != 1 %}s{% endif %}">
                        {% render 'mts-icon', name: 'star', size: 26 %}
                        <span class="sr-only">{{ i }} star{% if i != 1 %}s{% endif %}</span>
                      </label>
                    {%- endfor -%}
                  </div>
                </fieldset>

                <label class="pdp-rev__label">Your name
                  <input type="text" name="contact[name]" required autocomplete="name" value="{{ form.name }}">
                </label>
                <label class="pdp-rev__label">Email
                  <input type="email" name="contact[email]" required autocomplete="email"
                         inputmode="email" autocapitalize="none" spellcheck="false" value="{{ form.email }}">
                  <span class="pdp-rev__hint">Not published — we only use it if we need to check something.</span>
                </label>
                <label class="pdp-rev__label">Your review
                  <textarea name="contact[body]" rows="5" required
                            placeholder="How did it hold up? What did you use it for?">{{ form.body }}</textarea>
                </label>
                <div class="pdp-rev__actions">
                  <button type="submit" class="btn btn--brand btn--lg">Submit review</button>
                  <button type="button" class="btn btn--ghost" data-mts-rev-cancel>Cancel</button>
                </div>
              {%- endif -%}
            {%- endform -%}
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="pdp-desc pdp-ship" data-mts-panel="shipping" hidden>
        {{ section.settings.shipping_copy }}
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  {%- assign related = section.settings.related_collection | default: first_coll.handle -%}
  {%- assign related_coll = collections[related] -%}
  {%- if related_coll.products.size > 1 -%}
    <section class="section section--paper">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="section-head">
          <div class="section-title-wrap">
            <span class="eyebrow">{{ 'products.product.related_eyebrow' | t }}</span>
            <h2>{{ 'products.product.related' | t }}</h2>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="grid-products grid-products--fit">
          {%- assign shown = 0 -%}
          {%- for p in related_coll.products limit: 6 -%}
            {%- if p.handle != product.handle and shown < 4 -%}
              {% render 'mts-product-card', product: p, index: forloop.index0 %}
              {%- assign shown = shown | plus: 1 -%}
            {%- endif -%}
          {%- endfor -%}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%} lightbox {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="pdp-lightbox" data-mts-lightbox role="dialog" aria-modal="true"
       aria-label="{{ product.title | escape }} — image viewer" hidden>
    <button class="pdp-lightbox__close" data-mts-lightbox-close aria-label="Close image viewer">
      {% render 'mts-icon', name: 'close', size: 26 %}
    </button>
    <button class="pdp-lightbox__nav pdp-lightbox__nav--prev" data-mts-lightbox-prev aria-label="Previous image">
      {% render 'mts-icon', name: 'chevronRight', size: 28 %}
    </button>
    <figure class="pdp-lightbox__stage">
      {%- comment -%}
        src is set by mts-theme.js when the lightbox opens, and the stage is
        sized to the viewport by CSS — an intrinsic width/height here would be
        a fiction, so the rule is disabled for this tag rather than satisfied
        with made-up numbers.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {% # theme-check-disable ImgWidthAndHeight %}
      <img alt="{{ product.title | escape }}" data-mts-lightbox-img>
      {% # theme-check-enable ImgWidthAndHeight %}
      <figcaption class="num" data-mts-lightbox-count></figcaption>
    </figure>
    <button class="pdp-lightbox__nav pdp-lightbox__nav--next" data-mts-lightbox-next aria-label="Next image">
      {% render 'mts-icon', name: 'chevronRight', size: 28 %}
    </button>
  </div>
</main>

{%- comment -%}
  PRODUCT STRUCTURED DATA
  Both migration checklists list this as a launch blocker: "Product schema in
  theme: name, price, currency AUD, availability, SKU, brand, review rating"
  and "Schema and review stars validate in Rich Results Test". Nothing on the
  page emitted it, so products were eligible for none of it.

  Review stars in Shopping and organic results are the reason the 119 migrated
  reviews were worth carrying across at all — without aggregateRating here,
  Google never sees them. Only real data is emitted: no aggregateRating block
  is written for a product with no reviews, because inventing one is exactly
  what gets a site's rich results revoked.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  assign rev_items = product.metafields.reviews.items.value
  assign avail = 'https://schema.org/OutOfStock'
  if current_variant.available
    assign avail = 'https://schema.org/InStock'
  endif
-%}
<script type="application/ld+json">
{
  "@context": "https://schema.org/",
  "@type": "Product",
  "name": {{ product.title | strip_html | json }},
  "description": {{ product.description | strip_html | truncate: 900 | json }},
  "url": {{ shop.url | append: product.url | json }},
  {% if product.featured_media %}"image": {{ product.featured_media | image_url: width: 1200 | prepend: "https:" | json }},{% endif %}
  {% if product.vendor != blank %}"brand": { "@type": "Brand", "name": {{ product.vendor | json }} },{% endif %}
  {% if current_variant.sku != blank %}"sku": {{ current_variant.sku | json }},{% endif %}
  {% if current_variant.barcode != blank %}"gtin": {{ current_variant.barcode | json }},{% endif %}
  {% if rev_items and rev_items.size > 0 %}
  "aggregateRating": {
    "@type": "AggregateRating",
    "ratingValue": {{ rating | json }},
    "reviewCount": {{ rev_items.size | json }},
    "bestRating": "5",
    "worstRating": "1"
  },
  "review": [
    {%- for rv in rev_items limit: 20 -%}
    {
      "@type": "Review",
      "author": { "@type": "Person", "name": {{ rv.n | json }} },
      "datePublished": {{ rv.d | json }},
      "reviewBody": {{ rv.b | strip_html | json }},
      "reviewRating": { "@type": "Rating", "ratingValue": {{ rv.r | json }}, "bestRating": "5", "worstRating": "1" }
    }{% unless forloop.last %},{% endunless %}
    {%- endfor -%}
  ],
  {% endif %}
  "offers": {
    "@type": "Offer",
    "url": {{ shop.url | append: product.url | json }},
    "priceCurrency": {{ cart.currency.iso_code | json }},
    "price": {{ current_variant.price | divided_by: 100.0 | json }},
    "availability": {{ avail | json }},
    "itemCondition": "https://schema.org/NewCondition",
    "seller": { "@type": "Organization", "name": {{ shop.name | json }} }
  }
}
</script>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Product",
  "blocks": [{ "type": "@app" }],
  "settings": [
    { "type": "text", "id": "parent_label", "label": "Breadcrumb parent", "info": "e.g. \"Single-Sided Tapes\"." },
    { "type": "url", "id": "parent_url", "label": "Breadcrumb parent link" },
    { "type": "url", "id": "shipping_url", "label": "Shipping info link" },
    { "type": "url", "id": "returns_url", "label": "Returns link" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "related_collection", "label": "Related products collection handle", "info": "Leave blank to use the product's first collection." },
    {
      "type": "richtext",
      "id": "shipping_copy",
      "label": "Shipping & returns tab copy",
      "default": "<h3>Shipping &amp; delivery</h3><p>We deliver to every part of Australia. Orders are processed within 1–2 business days — placed before our daily cut-off, they're processed the same day. Every shipment includes tracking so you can follow your parcel in real time.</p><h3>Returns &amp; exchanges</h3><p>Incorrect items we send can be returned for exchange; items ordered by mistake can be returned at your expense. Faulty or damaged items are replaced free — file a claim within 2 days of delivery with photos. Items must be returned within 30 days of delivery, and refunds are processed within 7 days of receipt.</p>"
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
